From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 09:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] ntdll: Report hot writes only beside a read-only data page.

Patch 0986 reports a hot write beside a read-only page to the program,
as a write to that page, so that a program that protects pages to learn
when they change drops the protection. A code page counted as read-only
too. .NET's stubs keep a code page and the data page its code reads side
by side, and the runtime writes that data page all the time: after 256
writes in a host page, the next one was reported as an access violation
on the code page. .NET has no handler for it and ended the process
(Slay the Spire 2, a Godot .NET game, at its first Godot.Image).

A hot write beside an executable page is now treated as one beside an
inaccessible page: the host page keeps the union of its pages'
protections until one of them changes. Writes beside a read-only data
page are still reported.
---
 dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c | 19 ++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
index 1608c38fc2..2c7556af19 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c
@@ -5254,13 +5254,14 @@ static NTSTATUS grow_thread_stack_to( struct thread_data *data, char *page, stru
  *
  * Counts an access stepped over in a host page because a page in it is read-only
  * or inaccessible. Returns FALSE to step this one, until the host page has had
- * SUBPAGE_HOT_STEPS of them. Then a write beside a read-only page is reported
- * there instead (*report): the count starts again, so a program that keeps the
- * page read-only gets one report per SUBPAGE_HOT_STEPS writes and the rest are
- * stepped, not a loop. Any other hot access cannot be reported to the program
- * (a read, or a write beside an inaccessible page that the program never touches,
- * as a garbage collector's heap): the host page keeps the union of its pages'
- * protections, as without this scheme, until one of them changes.
+ * SUBPAGE_HOT_STEPS of them. Then a write beside a read-only page that holds no
+ * code is reported there instead (*report): the count starts again, so a program
+ * that keeps the page read-only gets one report per SUBPAGE_HOT_STEPS writes and
+ * the rest are stepped, not a loop. Any other hot access cannot be reported to
+ * the program (a read, a write beside an inaccessible page that the program never
+ * touches, as a garbage collector's heap, or a write beside a code page, as .NET's
+ * stubs, whose data page sits next to their code): the host page keeps the union
+ * of its pages' protections, as without this scheme, until one of them changes.
  */
 static BOOL subpage_hot( char *page, BOOL write, char **report )
 {
@@ -5280,7 +5281,7 @@ static BOOL subpage_hot( char *page, BOOL write, char **report )
     for (i = 0; write && i < host_page_size && !*report; i += page_size)
     {
         vprot = get_page_vprot( page + i );
-        if ((vprot & VPROT_COMMITTED) && !(vprot & (VPROT_GUARD | VPROT_WRITEWATCH)) &&
+        if ((vprot & VPROT_COMMITTED) && !(vprot & (VPROT_GUARD | VPROT_WRITEWATCH | VPROT_EXEC)) &&
             (get_unix_prot( vprot ) & PROT_READ) && !(get_unix_prot( vprot ) & PROT_WRITE))
             *report = page + i;
     }
@@ -5368,7 +5369,7 @@ NTSTATUS virtual_handle_fault( struct thread_data *data, EXCEPTION_RECORD *rec,
         else if (subpage_hot( page, err == EXCEPTION_WRITE_FAULT, &protected_page ))
         {
             /* Accesses beside a protected page come too often to step each one. Beside a
-             * read-only page, report a write to that page instead, as if this one had
+             * read-only data page, report a write to that page instead, as if this one had
              * touched it: a program that protects pages to learn when they change (an
              * emulator's recompiled code) then drops its protection, and the host page is
              * writable again. Otherwise give the host page the union of its pages'
